bdj: let the disc's own logger be switched on.

Retail BD-J discs built with the "radius" menu engines (Lionsgate's
com.lge.radius, Universal's com.uni.radius) carry a logger compiled in but
switched off - an obfuscated class with a static one-int-arg setter for a
minimum log level, shipped at 999 so nothing prints. Switched on, the disc
narrates itself: title changes, state machine transitions, resource loading,
and which playlist it decided to start.

That is the only direct view of a disc's own decisions. Everything else is
inference from libbluray's side. It is what identified why John Wick 1/3/4
and Hereditary never reached their menus: with a BD-J resume record present
the engine ends in a state whose only exit is a presentation that plays no
playlist, so nothing is ever demuxed and the overlay is never composited.

Off unless BD_DISC_LOG is set, and any failure to enable is logged and
swallowed, so a disc playing normally cannot be affected.

  BD_DISC_LOG=1        try the known engines
  BD_DISC_LOG=gv.aN    that class and setter, level 0
  BD_DISC_LOG=gv.aN=0  the same, level given explicitly

Kodi debug logging has to be on as well: this logger reaches the Kodi log as
CBlurayCallback::Logger, which is LOGDEBUG, so at Kodi's default level both
the enable line and everything the disc prints are dropped.

Names are obfuscated per authoring-tool build, so a disc outside the known
set needs the explicit form. Find it with javap on the disc's JAR: the setter
is a one-line "public static void x(int)" writing a static int that the
engine's log method compares the message level against.

